<!-- 📘 Ledger -->
A ledger is a record book of all transactions.

In blockchain, the ledger is digital

It is shared across all nodes

It is immutable (can’t be changed)

👉 Example:
If Alice sends money to Bob, that transaction is written in the ledger and copied to every node.

💡 Difference from bank ledger:

Bank → centralized

Blockchain → decentralized


<!-- 🌱 Genesis Block -->

The genesis block is the first block of a blockchain.

Block number: 0

Has no previous hash

Starting point of the entire chain

👉 Every blockchain starts from the genesis block.

Fun fact 😄
The Bitcoin genesis block contains a hidden newspaper headline.



<!-- 🧱 Block -->

A block is a container of data.

Each block contains:

Transaction data

Timestamp

Hash of the block

Hash of previous block

Blocks are linked together to form the blockchain.





<!-- 🔑 Hash -->

A hash is a fixed-length encrypted value.

Generated using a hash function

Even a tiny change in data → completely different hash

Used to secure and link blocks

Think of it as a digital fingerprint.




<!-- 🔗 Previous Hash -->

Each block stores the hash of the previous block

This creates the chain

If one block is altered → chain breaks ❌

This is what makes blockchain tamper-proof.





<!-- 🖥️ Node -->

A node is a computer connected to the blockchain network.

Stores a copy of the ledger

Verifies transactions

Maintains decentralization

More nodes = more security.




<!-- 🤝 Consensus -->

Consensus is how nodes agree on which transactions are valid.

Common mechanisms:

Proof of Work

Proof of Stake

Without consensus → no trust.





<!-- ⛏️ Mining / Validation -->

Mining → creating new blocks (PoW)

Validation → approving transactions (PoS)

Reward is usually given in cryptocurrency.





<!-- 📜 Smart Contract -->

A smart contract is a self-executing program on the blockchain.

Runs automatically when conditions are met

No middleman needed